        "# Task 1: Smart Buildings Dataset and Chapter 2 Probability\n",
        "\n",
        "**Dataset:** `combined_runs.csv` - 60 simulated control runs of the NeurIPS 2026 Smart Buildings challenge (6 buildings x 5 cities x 2 baseline policies, 4,032 five-minute steps each)."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "id": "Gqd1PB3fUzUT",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "Gqd1PB3fUzUT"
      },
      "source": [
        "# 0. Intro\n",
        "## Problem\n",
        "How likely is a 5-minute step of a simulated building-control run to be **heavily penalised** (reward below -0.1), and which conditions - occupancy, weather, controller policy, building - change that probability?\n",
        "\n",
        "## Why it matters\n",
        "This is the question behind the NeurIPS 2026 Smart Buildings control challenge. The answer tells us (1) which scenarios are hardest, (2) how to build fair train/validation/test splits (by building and run, not by row, because steps are autocorrelated) and (3) which baseline penalty rate a learned controller must beat.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Data in one paragraph\n",
        "One file, `combined_runs.csv`: 241,920 rows x 37 columns. One row = one 5-minute step of one simulation run. There are 60 runs = 6 buildings x 5 weather cities x 2 controller policies (`pid_water` and the noisy `noisy_pid_water`), each with 4,032 steps (14 days). The experiment for all probabilities is: *pick one step at random from the 241,920 equally likely steps*.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Objectives\n",
        "1. Understand and prepare the dataset (Sections 1-4).\n",
        "2. Apply every Chapter 2 concept to real questions: sample space and events, counting, axioms, complement, addition rule, conditional probability, multiplication rule, total probability, Bayes' theorem, independence (Section 5).\n",
        "3. Validate the main results with independent calculations: rule vs direct count vs an independent re-implementation, block bootstrap, preprocessing and threshold sensitivity (Section 6).\n",
        "4. Turn the numbers into insights, state the limitations and the use in the competition (Section 7).\n",
        "\n",
        "## Analysis steps\n",
        "Load and check the data -> clean and derive columns (with a before/after row log) -> define events (OCC, HP, NOISY, COLD, ...) -> apply the probability rules, one application at a time (question, events and assumptions, rule, calculation, interpretation) -> validate three ways -> summarise findings.\n",
        "\n",
        "## How to read this notebook\n",
        "Every code cell is preceded by a text cell saying what is being done and why. Random numbers use seed 42. Run **Runtime > Restart and run all**; no step needs a manual edit. Rule-based and direct-count results are asserted to match exactly."
      ]
    },
