        "## 3.3 Preprocessing decisions and their effect on probabilities\n",
        "\n",
        "- **Nothing removed.** Section 2.4 found no missing or duplicate rows, so all probabilities use all 241,920 time steps. Results describe these 60 runs only.\n",
        "- **Constant and redundant columns dropped from the analysis frame.** They cannot form a non-trivial event, so no probability changes. `df_raw` keeps them.\n",
        "- **Extreme rewards (down to -1.70) are kept.** They are valid simulator output. Section 6.5 shows how the main probabilities move if the worst 1% is removed.\n",
        "- **Thresholds are analyst choices.** The cut-offs used for events (reward below -0.1, 10 °C, 25 °C, 300 K, 290 K) are our choices, not official limits. Section 6.5 varies the reward threshold.\n",
        "- **Terminology.** The 241,920 rows are time steps, not independent samples, because consecutive steps within a run are autocorrelated."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "id": "cdeda99e",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "cdeda99e"
      },
      "source": [
        "# 4. Events\n",
        "\n",
        "**Textbook link.** In Sec. 2-1.3, an *event* is a subset of the sample space S. Our S is the 241,920 time steps, so each event below is a True/False column: a step belongs to the event when its value is True. In Sec. 2-2, with equally likely outcomes, P(E) = (number of steps in E) / |S| = the mean of the column.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Assumption.** Picking a time step at random makes all 241,920 steps equally likely. The steps are not independent, because consecutive steps in a run are autocorrelated, so we always say \"time steps\" and not \"samples\".\n",
        "\n",
        "**Inequalities.** We use strict inequalities consistently (for example OCC is occupancy > 0, HP is reward < -0.1)."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "id": "a5dea644",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "a5dea644"
      },
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "q75 = df.rc_electricity_energy_cost.quantile(0.75)          # about 0.2907\n",
        "\n",
        "df[\"OCC\"]   = df.occupancy > 0                  # occupied step\n",
        "df[\"HP\"]    = df.reward < -0.1                  # high-penalty step\n",
        "df[\"NOISY\"] = df.policy == \"noisy_pid_water\"    # noisy controller\n",
        "df[\"COLD\"]  = df.outside_air_temp_K < 283.15    # below 10 C\n",
        "df[\"HOT\"]   = df.outside_air_temp_K > 298.15    # above 25 C\n",
        "df[\"HE\"]    = df.rc_electricity_energy_cost > q75   # high electricity cost (top 25%)\n",
        "df[\"ZH\"]    = df.zone_temp_max_K > 300          # hottest zone above 300 K\n",
        "df[\"ZC\"]    = df.zone_temp_min_K < 290          # coldest zone below 290 K\n",
        "df[\"WD\"]    = df.dow < 5                        # weekday\n",
        "\n",
        "for c in [\"atlanta\", \"denver\", \"fairbanks\", \"phoenix\", \"singapore\"]:\n",
        "    df[\"C_\" + c] = df.city == c                 # city events (a partition of S)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "id": "3eb649fe",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "3eb649fe"
      },
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "med_z = df.drop_duplicates(\"building\").run_n_zones.median()      # 46.5, median over the 6 buildings\n",
        "df[\"BIG\"] = df.run_n_zones > med_z                               # buildings 2, 4, 5\n",
        "df[\"F2\"], df[\"F3\"], df[\"F4\"] = [df.run_n_floors == k for k in (2, 3, 4)]\n",
        "\n",
        "rm = df.groupby(\"run_id\").run_mean_reward.first()                # one value per run\n",
        "df[\"BAD_RUN\"] = df.run_id.map(rm < rm.median())                  # run scores below the median"
      ]
    },
