        "## 4.4 Data limitations\n",
        "\n",
        "These results describe 60 simulated runs of two baseline controllers (`pid_water` and `noisy_pid_water`), not real buildings and not learned policies.\n",
        "\n",
        "- **Only 6 building designs.** Building-level results (size, floors) rest on 6 points, and building identity is confounded with size, so a difference between buildings cannot be attributed to size alone.\n",
        "- **Short runs.** Each run covers 14 days (4,032 steps) in this file, and each building uses one weather file per city.\n",
        "- **Autocorrelated steps.** Consecutive steps in a run are strongly related, so the effective sample size is far below 241,920. We therefore say \"time steps\", not \"independent samples\", and every probability is a relative frequency over these steps (Sec. 2-2), not an estimate for new buildings or new weather.\n",
        "- **Repeated columns.** The `run_*` columns repeat on every row, so run-level and building-level statistics use the 60-row `runs` and 6-row `bld` tables.\n",
        "- **No terminating runs.** `done` is 0 everywhere, so no run ends early and no step carries an end-of-episode outcome.\n",
        "- **Threshold dependence.** Every event depends on thresholds we chose (reward below -0.1, 10 °C, 25 °C, 300 K, 290 K, the 75th percentile of electricity cost), not on official limits. Changing a threshold changes the probability, and Section 6.5 tests this.\n",
        "- **Training scenarios only.** The competition scores policies on hidden buildings and different weather, so conclusions drawn from these 6 training buildings may not carry over to the test set.\n",
        "- **Effective sample size.** The honest sample sizes are 840 run-days (60 runs × 14 days), 60 runs and 30 paired runs, not 241,920."
      ]
    },
