        "### 5.4b Application 5: `done` and the run-level model\n",
        "\n",
        "**Question.** A natural extra outcome for a control run would be \"the episode ends early\" (the `done` flag). Does that concept apply, and if not, what is a meaningful alternative?\n",
        "\n",
        "**Why the direct concept does not apply.** `done` is 0 in every row, so P(episode ends) = 0 exactly and there is no event to model. We say this explicitly and use an alternative.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Alternative: runs as the sample space.** Take the 60 runs as outcomes (equally likely) and use the run-level score `run_mean_reward` from the 60-row `runs` table (never averaged over rows, which would count each run 4,032 times).\n",
        "- Event BAD_RUN = {`run_mean_reward` below the median of the 60 runs}. By construction P(BAD_RUN) = 30/60 = 0.5.\n",
        "- Event NOISY = noisy-policy run. We compare P(BAD_RUN | NOISY) with P(BAD_RUN | clean) using counts of runs.\n",
        "- A paired check: in each of the 30 (building, weather) pairs, does the noisy run score lower than its clean twin?\n",
        "- The step-based quantity p_run = P(HP | OCC) per run (share of occupied steps that are heavily penalised), to see how much a single pooled probability hides.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Assumption.** Runs are treated as equally likely; there are only 60 of them, so every run-level probability is a multiple of 1/60."
