        "**Interpretation.**\n",
        "- *`done` gives nothing to model*: no run ever terminates, so P(episode ends) = 0. The honest replacement is to study the *run score*.\n",
        "- *Policy matters at run level.* Of the 30 runs scoring below the median, 19 are noisy-policy runs: P(BAD_RUN | noisy) = 19/30 = **0.633** against P(BAD_RUN | clean) = 11/30 = **0.367**. In a like-for-like comparison (same building and weather), the noisy run scores lower than its clean twin in **27 of 30 pairs (0.900)**.\n",
        "- *But policy alone does not decide a run.* The scores span from -0.016 (building_train_5, fairbanks_5, clean policy, the best) to -0.301 (building_train_1, fairbanks_1, clean policy, the worst). Both extremes are *clean-policy Fairbanks* runs, so building and weather can outweigh the policy.\n",
        "- *A pooled probability hides this spread.* p_run = P(HP | OCC) runs from 0.000 to 0.999 across the 60 runs (mean 0.506, close to the pooled 0.503; the two differ slightly because runs have different numbers of occupied steps). This motivates the run-level validation in Section 6.4: the honest uncertainty comes from having only 60 runs, not 241,920 rows."
      ]
