        "## 5.5 Application 6: Conditional probability (who gets penalised, and when?)\n",
        "*Textbook: Sec. 2-4 (conditional probability)*\n",
        "\n",
        "**Question.** Are heavily penalised steps mainly an occupied-building phenomenon, and do cold weather or building size change that?\n",
        "\n",
        "**Events and assumptions.** OCC (occupancy > 0), HP (reward < -0.1), COLD (outside air < 10 C), BIG (more than 46.5 zones: buildings 2, 4, 5). The experiment is the usual one: pick one of the 241,920 time steps at random, all equally likely. The steps are not independent observations, so every result is a relative frequency over these 60 runs.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Rule and why it fits.** For equally likely outcomes, P(A | B) = P(A and B) / P(B) = |A and B| / |B|: once we know B happened, the sample space shrinks from S to B. This is the right tool because the question is \"given people are present, how often is the reward low?\". We write both directions in words next to every number, because P(HP | OCC) and P(OCC | HP) answer different questions (the second one is Bayes, Application 9)."
