        "## 5.7 Application 8: Total probability\n",
        "*Textbook: Sec. 2-5 (total probability rule)*\n",
        "\n",
        "**Question.** Can we recover the overall penalty probability P(HP) from group-level rates alone, and which groups carry the risk?\n",
        "\n",
        "**Events and assumptions.** A *partition* is a set of mutually exclusive events that together cover S. We use four: the 5 cities, the 2 policies, the 3 floor counts (F2, F3, F4) and the 6 buildings. All of them are fixed by the design of the file (equal numbers of runs), so the weights P(group) are known exactly.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Rule and why it fits.** If B1, ..., Bk partition S, then P(HP) = sum over i of P(HP | Bi) P(Bi). It fits because group-level penalty rates are what an organiser would report per scenario, and the rule shows how they combine into one number; it is also the **denominator of Bayes' theorem** in Application 9. We compare each partition with the direct P(HP): they must agree exactly."
      ]
