        "## 5.8 Application 9: Bayes' theorem\n",
        "*Textbook: Sec. 2-7 (Bayes' theorem)*\n",
        "\n",
        "**Question.** A step is observed to be heavily penalised. What does that tell us about the *situation*: was the building occupied, which policy was running, in which city are we, and in what kind of building?\n",
        "\n",
        "**Events and assumptions.** Hypotheses H: OCC, NOISY, each city, F4, BIG. Evidence E: HP. Priors P(H) are the shares of steps; likelihoods P(HP | H) come from Applications 6 and 8. At run level the evidence is BAD_RUN (run score below the median) and the experiment changes to *runs* (60 outcomes). Same equally likely assumption.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Rule and why it fits.** Bayes' theorem reverses a conditional: P(H | E) = P(E | H) P(H) / P(E), where the denominator P(E) = sum of P(E | Hi) P(Hi) is the total probability of Application 8. It fits because the data naturally give \"penalty rate in each situation\", while an analyst who sees a penalty wants \"how likely is each situation\". We verify every posterior against the direct count |H and E| / |E|."
      ]
    },
