      "source": [
        "**Interpretation.** Seeing a penalty is **strong evidence for \"occupied\"**: P(OCC | HP) = 0.5029 x 0.3735 / 0.2007 = **0.936**, up from the prior 0.3735 (the same 0.936 as the direct count). It is only **weak evidence for the noisy policy** (0.50 to 0.566), because penalties happen under both controllers. For the city, the posterior simply re-weights the equal prior by each city's penalty rate: Fairbanks 0.272 and Phoenix 0.246 become the most likely places, Atlanta 0.132 the least. A penalty slightly lowers belief in \"big building\" (0.50 to 0.447) and \"4 floors\" (0.167 to 0.142), consistent with Application 6. At run level, a below-median run is noisy with probability 0.633 versus the prior 0.5.\n",
        "\n",
        "In plain words: the prior is what we believe before looking at the reward; the posterior is what we believe after seeing a low one. Bayes only reverses the direction of the conditional; it describes association in this simulated data and does not prove that occupancy or the policy *causes* the penalty."
      ]
    },
