        "## 5.9 Application 10: Independence\n",
        "*Textbook: Sec. 2-6 (independence)*\n",
        "\n",
        "**Question.** Which conditions in the simulator are independent of each other, and which are not? In particular: are weather and occupancy independent of the controller policy (which would validate the paired design), and does the policy matter only when people are present?\n",
        "\n",
        "**Events and assumptions.** OCC, HP, NOISY, COLD, HE (high electricity cost), BIG, F4 and one building event. Same equally likely time steps.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Rule and why it fits.** A and B are independent when P(A and B) = P(A) P(B), equivalently P(B | A) = P(B). We compare the observed joint probability with the product of the two individual probabilities using the difference P(A and B) - P(A) P(B). A difference of zero matches the independence rule; a positive difference means the events occur together more often than the rule predicts, and a negative difference means they occur together less often. For conditional independence, make the same comparison within a subset, such as occupied steps only.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Uncertainty.** With n = 241,920, a row-level standard error would be misleadingly small because steps within runs are related over time. We therefore use a **day-block bootstrap band**: resample whole run-days (840 blocks, 2,000 resamples, seed 42) and calculate a 95% band for the difference. A band that contains zero is *consistent with independence*; it does not prove independence. Building-level events (BIG, F4, one building) take only 6 distinct values, so their conclusions describe these six buildings rather than a general population.\n"
      ]
    },
