        "**Interpretation.**\n",
        "- *By design.* For OCC vs NOISY, COLD vs NOISY and BIG vs NOISY, P(A and B) equals P(A)P(B): each building/weather pairing has both policies, and the paired policies share timestamps, occupancy and outside temperature (checked in 2.4). This supports the paired simulation design: the two policies were compared under matching conditions.\n",
        "- *Positive dependence.* For OCC vs HP, the observed joint probability (about 0.188) is above the independence product (about 0.075). High electricity cost is also more common with OCC than its independence product predicts. NOISY vs HP and COLD vs HP show smaller positive differences.\n",
        "- *Negative dependence.* For OCC vs COLD, P(A and B) is below P(A)P(B), because occupied hours are mostly daytime while cold outside air is mostly at night.\n",
        "- *Conditional independence.* For NOISY vs HP given OCC, the observed joint probability is above its product. Given an empty building, the difference is near zero and its day-block band contains zero: these data are consistent with policy and HP being independent during unoccupied steps. This is conditional independence in action: the overall relationship can differ from the relationship inside a subgroup.\n",
        "- *Building-level pairs.* BIG vs OCC, BIG vs HP, F4 vs HP and one building vs OCC use only 6 distinct buildings (and one 4-floor building). Treat them as descriptions of these buildings, not general laws. A small difference can be consistent with independence when its block-bootstrap band contains zero.\n"
