        "### 5.9b Mutually exclusive is not independent, and mutual independence of three events\n",
        "*Textbook: Sec. 2-6 (independence), Sec. 2-3 (mutually exclusive events)*\n",
        "\n",
        "**Question.** (a) COLD and HOT never happen together; does that make them independent? (b) For three events, is checking each pair enough to call all three events mutually independent?\n",
        "\n",
        "**Rules and why they fit.**\n",
        "- *Mutually exclusive versus independent.* Mutually exclusive events with positive probabilities are dependent. If A happens, B cannot, so P(B | A) = 0 while P(B) > 0. Independence requires P(A and B) = P(A)P(B). For COLD and HOT, the joint probability is 0 but the product of their probabilities is greater than 0, so they are not independent.\n",
        "- *Mutual independence of three events.* A, B and C are mutually independent only when the independence rule holds for all three pairs **and** for all three together: P(A and B) = P(A)P(B), P(A and C) = P(A)P(C), P(B and C) = P(B)P(C), and P(A and B and C) = P(A)P(B)P(C). The table below compares each joint probability with the corresponding product. Application 10 separately uses day-block bootstrap bands when judging pairwise results."
      ]
