      "source": [
        "**Interpretation.** (a) COLD and HOT are mutually exclusive: they never happen together, so P(COLD and HOT) = 0. But P(COLD)P(HOT) is about 0.1093, not 0. Because the joint probability does not equal the product, COLD and HOT are dependent. Equivalently, P(HOT | COLD) = 0 while P(HOT) = 0.386: knowing the step is cold tells us it is not hot.\n",
        "\n",
        "(b) In Triple 1, NOISY is independent of OCC and of COLD individually, and it is also independent of the combined event OCC and COLD. However, OCC and COLD are dependent: P(OCC and COLD) = 0.0777, while P(OCC)P(COLD) = 0.1058. So the three events are **not** mutually independent. In particular, P(OCC and COLD and NOISY) equals P(OCC and COLD)P(NOISY), but it does not equal P(OCC)P(COLD)P(NOISY).\n",
        "\n",
        "Triple 2 fails because OCC, COLD and HP are dependent in each pair, and the three-event product rule also fails. Mutual independence requires the independence rule to hold for **all three pairs and the three-event combination**. Checking only some pairs is not enough."
      ]
