      "source": [
        "**Reading the findings.** Findings 1-3 describe *where* the penalty probability is high, finding 4 shows the comparison is fair, finding 5 says how sure we can be, and finding 6 rules out a tempting explanation. Together they say: **occupied hours, extreme climates and the noisy policy are the hard cases; building size is not.** All results are descriptive associations. The data cannot say that occupancy or the noisy policy *causes* penalties, only that the probability is higher in those conditions.\n",
        "\n",
        "## 7.3 Where a concept does not apply directly\n",
        "The column `done` is always 0 (no episode ends early), so the idea of a \"rejected\" step has no counterpart here. We used run-level and building-level events instead (Applications 3 and 5), which is what the data actually supports.\n",
        "\n",
        "## 7.4 Assumptions and limitations\n",
        "- **Simulation, not reality.** Two baseline controllers (clean and noisy PID), not real buildings and not a learned agent.\n",
        "- **Few independent units.** Only 6 building designs, one weather file per (building, city), 14-day windows. Size and floor results rest on 6 points.\n",
        "- **Autocorrelation.** Neighbouring steps are alike, so the effective sample is 60 runs / 840 run-days. Block bootstraps (Section 6.4) correct the uncertainty; the point estimates are unaffected.\n",
        "- **Analyst thresholds.** HP (reward < -0.1), COLD (< 283.15 K), BIG (> 46.5 zones) are our choices; Section 6.5 shows other thresholds change the level but not the ordering.\n",
        "- **Fixed schedule.** Occupancy follows a Monday-Friday timetable, so \"OCC\" is partly a time-of-day effect.\n",
        "- **Descriptive only.** Probabilities describe this dataset; they do not prove causes.\n",
        "\n",
        "## 7.5 Competition relevance\n",
        "1. **Splits.** Split by building (C(6,2) = 15 hold-out pairs) to avoid leakage between rows of the same run and to get honest scores.\n",
        "2. **Hard scenarios.** Penalty probability by city and occupancy shows where controllers struggle; a curriculum can go from mild climates to extreme ones.\n",
        "3. **Baseline benchmark.** A learned controller should beat the occupied-step penalty rate of the clean PID baseline and, at minimum, of the noisy one (finding 2).\n",
        "4. **Coverage.** Independence results show which conditions to vary to cover the space; occupied hours deserve the most training weight.\n",
        "5. **Compute.** Larger buildings take much longer to simulate, so budget compute for them and start training on small buildings."
      ]
